---

Canonical schema: `system/substance-page-schema.md`.  
**Worked examples:** `docs/substances/bioactive-compounds/carotenoids/astaxanthin.md` (Highlights) and `docs/substances/nutrients/micronutrients/minerals/trace/copper.md` (Highlights + Advanced Nutrition + Therapeutic Area Research).  
References follow the same bibliographic convention as food pages (`system/food-page-schema.md`).

- Tag each food page with the substances it contains.
- Tag the page with "Substance" so that it appears in index.md.
- Tag with "Bioactive", "Metabolite", "Nutrient" and any other substance classifications such as "Mineral", "Vitamin", "Fatty Acid" etc.
- Tag with the **BRS hub tag names** used on biological-target documents (e.g. `Inflammation & Oxidative Stress`) when a mapping is evidenced.
- Tag with the tags for any foods that contain this substance.
- In the front matter, `list_image:` one of `/img/icons/substance.svg` `/img/icons/nutrient.svg` `/img/icons/metabolite.svg` `/img/icons/bioactive.svg` (fallback if InChIKey is not available).
- In the front matter, `inchikey:` (optional). If provided, the chemical structure image will be fetched from PubChem. Example: `inchikey: MDJHJOXOBXJZLA-UHFFFAOYSA-N`.
- In the front matter, `mechanisms:` explains how the substance interacts with Biological Targets. Keys must match BRS hub tag labels.

## Canonical section order

1. **Overview** — editorial synthesis: identity, distinctive biological/nutritional relevance, BRAIN relevance, and major evidence qualification. Do not enumerate mechanisms for coverage. Inline numeric citations `[1]`, `[2]`.
2. **Dietary Origin** — supported relationship paths only: direct occurrence, dietary precursor, microbial/endogenous formation, food-matrix/bioavailability context, and direct supplemental/concentrated exposure. Never equate direct occurrence with precursor-supported formation.
3. **Research Spotlights & Evidence Checks** — optional evidence presentations. Use `<EvidencePresentation>` and retain `exposureContext`; do not create duplicate evidence records.
4. **Recipes** — `<SubstanceRecipes tag="the substance" />`
5. **Foods** — `<SubstanceFoods tag="the substance" />`; relationship labels must distinguish contains from precursor/support edges.
6. **Biological Regulatory Systems** — compact cited BRS table, preferably resolving through evidence-qualified PM/KC relationships. Tag-driven `<SubstanceMatrix />` is legacy fallback. Deeper biology goes in `<AdvancedNutrition>`; condition-specific evidence goes in `<TherapeuticAreaResearch>`. Headings inside those slots must be HTML so they stay out of the Highlights TOC.
7. **References** — canonical author/year, linked-title, explanation format. **No bullet prefix.** Each line:

```text
[n] {Author et al.} ({Year}). [{Full paper title}](/docs/papers/BRAIN-Diet-References#citationKey). {Why this source matters on this Substance page.}
```

`Key Compound Highlights` is not a canonical standalone section. Resolve useful material into Overview, Dietary Origin, Research Spotlights/Evidence Checks, or an advanced evidence view.

Every citation key must exist in `static/bibtex/BRAIN-diet.bib`. Plain-text-only reference lines are invalid.

Attribute evidence at the level studied (isolated compound vs glycoside vs extract vs food). Do not copy family-level or food-level citations onto an isolated-compound page unless that paper actually studied the compound.
